3.4.3 Composting
Composting is not included as a materials management pathway for paper products. Although paper products are composted, the composting factor in WARM, described in the Composting chapter, assumes a generic compost mix, rather than looking at materials in isolation. It is not currently known what effect adding paper would have at a composting site, including whether the GHG emissions/sequestration would be altered or whether the carbon/nitrogen ratio would be affected.
3.4.4 Combustion
Combusting paper products results in emissions of both CO2 and N2O. Because carbon in paper products is considered to be biogenic,20 CO2 emissions from combustion are not considered in WARM. The N2O emissions, however, are included in WARM’s GHG emission factors for paper products.
Transporting paper products to combustion facilities also results in GHG emissions from the combustion of fossil fuels in vehicles. Finally, electricity produced from waste combustion energy recovery is used to offset the need for electricity production at power plants, consequently reducing the power sector’s consumption of fossil fuels. WARM takes this into account by calculating an avoided utility emission offset.21
